package cajeta.net.dns;

import cajeta.lang.String;
import cajeta.net.NetException;

/**
 * Name resolution **failed for a reason other than "no such name"** —
 * the non-`NXDOMAIN` arm of a [`Dns.resolve`](Dns.cajeta) lookup (plan
 * item **NET-2.5**). Where
 * [`UnknownHostException`](UnknownHostException.cajeta) is a definitive
 * negative ("this name does not exist"), this is everything else the
 * resolver can report: a transient server failure, a non-recoverable
 * server error, an unsupported family, a local resource/flag problem, or
 * an unknown service.
 *
 * ## When it is raised
 *
 * [`NetErrors.fromResolveErrno`](../NetErrors.cajeta) maps every
 * normalized `cajeta_resolve_err` ordinal that is **not** NONAME (1) or
 * NODATA (2) — i.e. the transport/server/local-error bucket — to this
 * subtype (mirrored from
 * [`runtime/native/cajeta_net_getaddrinfo.c`](../../../../native/cajeta_net_getaddrinfo.c)):
 *
 *   - `AGAIN` (3) — `EAI_AGAIN` / `WSATRY_AGAIN`: transient, **retry may
 *     succeed** (a temporary nameserver failure). This is the one
 *     `ResolutionFailed` cause that is *not* cacheable as a negative.
 *   - `FAIL` (4) — `EAI_FAIL` / `WSANO_RECOVERY`: non-recoverable server
 *     failure (`SERVFAIL`).
 *   - `FAMILY` (5) — requested address family unsupported.
 *   - `MEMORY` (6) — resolver out of memory.
 *   - `SYSTEM` (7) — a system error (POSIX `EAI_SYSTEM`; `errno` set).
 *   - `BADFLAGS` (8) — invalid hints/flags (a caller / bridge bug).
 *   - `SERVICE` (9) — the requested service/port is not supported.
 *   - `OTHER` (99) — any unmapped `EAI_*` code.
 *
 * The transient vs. permanent distinction is preserved in
 * [`resolveErrno`](#resolveErrno) so a retry policy can act on `AGAIN`
 * specifically.
 *
 * ## The `kind` / `resolveErrno` split
 *
 * Like the other higher-layer subtypes (see
 * [`docs/stdlib/net/Errors.md`](../../../../../docs/stdlib/net/Errors.md)),
 * this does **not** classify a socket `cajeta_net_err`, so its inherited
 * [`kind`](../NetException.cajeta) is the coarse `KIND_OTHER` (99) — its
 * *type* is the primary discriminant — and the exact
 * `cajeta_resolve_err` ordinal rides on
 * [`resolveErrno`](#resolveErrno).
 *
 * Constructed directly (no `super(...)` chaining — the compiler still
 * rejects it for `Throwable` subtypes; the subtype sets the inherited
 * `message`/`cause`/`kind` and its own field, exactly as
 * [`UnknownHostException`](UnknownHostException.cajeta) does).
 *
 * Recoverable, not Unrecoverable: callers either catch it (or the
 * [`NetException`](../NetException.cajeta) root) or declare it in their
 * throws clause, mirroring the
 * [`cajeta.io.file`](../../io/file/IoException.cajeta) discipline.
 */
public class ResolutionFailedException extends NetException {
    /**
     * The normalized `cajeta_resolve_err` ordinal that classified this
     * failure — one of `AGAIN` (3), `FAIL` (4), `FAMILY` (5), `MEMORY`
     * (6), `SYSTEM` (7), `BADFLAGS` (8), `SERVICE` (9), or `OTHER` (99).
     * `AGAIN` (3) is the only transient cause (retry may succeed). The
     * coarse [`kind`](../NetException.cajeta) is `KIND_OTHER` (99); this
     * is the precise DNS discriminant.
     */
    public int32 resolveErrno;

    public ResolutionFailedException(String message, int32 resolveErrno) {
        this.message = message;
        this.cause = 0;
        this.kind = 99;   // NetException.KIND_OTHER — DNS failures are discriminated by type, not errno
        this.resolveErrno = resolveErrno;
    }
}
